\pard\sl240\slmult1\f0\fs22\lang9\u-10179?\u-9000?\f1  EPISODE 1 \f2\emdash\f1  What Trauma Does to a Child\rquote s Brain\par
Key Message\par
Trauma shifts the brain into survival mode.\par
\par
What This Means for Your Child\par
They may react quickly or strongly.\par
\par
They may struggle to listen or sit still.\par
\par
Their brain is focused on safety, not learning.\par
\par
They are not choosing these reactions.\par
\par
Try This at Home\par
Lower your voice and slow your movements.\par
\par
Offer simple choices (\ldblquote blue cup or red cup?\rdblquote ).\par
\par
Create a calm space your child can go to.\par
\par
Use short, clear sentences.\par
\par
Reflection Questions\par
When does my child seem most overwhelmed?\par
\par
What situations make them feel unsafe?\par
\par
How do I respond in those moments?\par
\par
Notes\par
(Blank space for parents)\par
\par
\f0\u-10179?\u-9000?\f1  EPISODE 2 \f2\emdash\f1  Why Trauma Looks Like Misbehaviour\par
Key Message\par
Behaviour is communication.\par
\par
What This Means for Your Child\par
Lying may be fear of getting in trouble.\par
\par
Stealing food may be fear of not having enough.\par
\par
Tantrums may be emotional overload.\par
\par
\ldblquote Defiance\rdblquote  may be a survival response.\par
\par
Try This at Home\par
Ask yourself: \ldblquote What is this behaviour protecting?\rdblquote\par
\par
Stay curious instead of reacting quickly.\par
\par
Praise honesty gently and consistently.\par
\par
Provide predictable routines.\par
\par
Reflection Questions\par
What behaviours do I find hardest to understand?\par
\par
What might my child be trying to tell me?\par
\par
How can I respond with curiosity?\par
\par
Notes\par
(Blank space)\par
\par
\f0\u-10179?\u-9000?\f1  EPISODE 3 \f2\emdash\f1  Fight, Flight, Freeze, Fawn\par
Key Message\par
These are survival responses, not choices.\par
\par
What This Means for Your Child\par
Fight: yelling, hitting, arguing\par
\par
Flight: running away, hiding\par
\par
Freeze: shutting down, blank stare\par
\par
Fawn: people\f3\_\f1 pleasing, over\f3\_\f1 agreeing\par
\par
These responses happen automatically.\par
\par
Try This at Home\par
Identify your child\rquote s main stress response.\par
\par
Stay close and calm during reactions.\par
\par
Use grounding techniques (breathing, counting).\par
\par
Reduce demands during overwhelm.\par
\par
Reflection Questions\par
Which stress response does my child use most?\par
\par
How do I usually react?\par
\par
What could help my child feel safer?\par
\par
Notes\par
(Blank space)\par
\par
\f0\u-10179?\u-9000?\f1  EPISODE 4 \f2\emdash\f1  Emotional Regulation & Meltdowns\par
Key Message\par
Children borrow your calm.\par
\par
What This Means for Your Child\par
They cannot calm down alone.\par
\par
Their brain needs your steady presence.\par
\par
Teaching doesn\rquote t work during a meltdown.\par
\par
Connection comes before correction.\par
\par
Try This at Home\par
Take a slow breath before responding.\par
\par
Sit beside your child instead of talking at them.\par
\par
Use simple phrases: \ldblquote I\rquote m here. You\rquote re safe.\rdblquote\par
\par
After they calm, talk about feelings.\par
\par
Reflection Questions\par
How do I feel during my child\rquote s meltdowns?\par
\par
What helps me stay calm?\par
\par
What co\f3\_\f1 regulation strategies can I try?\par
\par
Notes\par
(Blank space)\par
\par
\f0\u-10179?\u-9000?\f1  EPISODE 5 \f2\emdash\f1  Attachment & Trust\par
Key Message\par
Testing is checking for safety.\par
\par
What This Means for Your Child\par
They may push you away to see if you stay.\par
\par
They may cling tightly when scared.\par
\par
They may struggle to trust adults.\par
\par
Consistency builds safety.\par
\par
Try This at Home\par
Keep routines predictable.\par
\par
Follow through on promises.\par
\par
Stay calm when they test boundaries.\par
\par
Offer connection even when they reject it.\par
\par
Reflection Questions\par
How does my child test for safety?\par
\par
How do I respond?\par
\par
What helps my child feel secure?\par
\par
Notes\par
(Blank space)\par
\par
\f0\u-10179?\u-9000?\f1  EPISODE 6 \f2\emdash\f1  Developmental Delays & Regression\par
Key Message\par
Trauma can freeze development.\par
\par
What This Means for Your Child\par
They may act younger than their age.\par
\par
Regression is a sign of stress, not immaturity.\par
\par
They may need younger\f3\_\f1 child support.\par
\par
Safety helps development restart.\par
\par
Try This at Home\par
Meet them at their emotional age.\par
\par
Offer comfort items without shame.\par
\par
Use play to rebuild skills.\par
\par
Celebrate small steps forward.\par
\par
Reflection Questions\par
What younger needs is my child showing me?\par
\par
How can I support those needs?\par
\par
What progress have I noticed?\par
\par
Notes\par
(Blank space)\par
\par
\f0\u-10179?\u-9000?\f1  EPISODE 7 \f2\emdash\f1  Trauma & Learning\par
Key Message\par
A stressed brain can\rquote t learn.\par
\par
What This Means for Your Child\par
They may forget instructions.\par
\par
They may struggle with homework.\par
\par
They may avoid challenges.\par
\par
Safety improves learning.\par
\par
Try This at Home\par
Create a calm homework space.\par
\par
Break tasks into small steps.\par
\par
Use movement breaks.\par
\par
Praise effort, not results.\par
\par
Reflection Questions\par
When does my child learn best?\par
\par
What stresses them during learning?\par
\par
How can I make learning feel safe?\par
\par
Notes\par
(Blank space)\par
\par
\f0\u-10179?\u-9000?\f1  EPISODE 8 \f2\emdash\f1  Sensory Overload\par
Key Message\par
Some children feel the world more intensely.\par
\par
What This Means for Your Child\par
Loud noises may feel painful.\par
\par
Touch may feel threatening.\par
\par
Crowds may overwhelm them.\par
\par
Sensory overload is not misbehaviour.\par
\par
Try This at Home\par
Offer headphones or quiet spaces.\par
\par
Use soft lighting.\par
\par
Reduce sensory input during stress.\par
\par
Let them choose clothing textures.\par
\par
Reflection Questions\par
What sensory triggers affect my child?\par
\par
How do they show overwhelm?\par
\par
What helps them feel grounded?\par
\par
Notes\par
(Blank space)\par
\par
\f0\u-10179?\u-9000?\f1  EPISODE 9 \f2\emdash\f1  Triggers\par
Key Message\par
Triggers are reminders of past danger.\par
\par
What This Means for Your Child\par
Their body reacts before they think.\par
\par
Triggers can be sounds, smells, tones, or situations.\par
\par
They may panic, freeze, or shut down.\par
\par
Predictability reduces triggers.\par
\par
Try This at Home\par
Identify common triggers.\par
\par
Give warnings before transitions.\par
\par
Use grounding tools (breathing, sensory items).\par
\par
Stay calm and reassuring.\par
\par
Reflection Questions\par
What triggers have I noticed?\par
\par
How does my child react?\par
\par
How can I reduce unexpected stress?\par
\par
Notes\par
(Blank space)\par
\par
\f0\u-10179?\u-9000?\f1  EPISODE 10 \f2\emdash\f1  Routine Builds Safety\par
Key Message\par
Predictability calms the nervous system.\par
\par
What This Means for Your Child\par
Routines reduce anxiety.\par
\par
They help children feel in control.\par
\par
Transitions are easier with warnings.\par
\par
Visual schedules support understanding.\par
\par
Try This at Home\par
Create a simple morning routine.\par
\par
Use a visual schedule.\par
\par
Give 2\f3\_\f1 minute warnings before transitions.\par
\par
Keep bedtime consistent.\par
\par
Reflection Questions\par
What routines help my child feel safe?\par
\par
Where do we need more predictability?\par
\par
What small routine can I add this week?\par
\par
Notes\par
(Blank space)\par
\par
\f0\u-10179?\u-9000?\f1  EPISODE 11 \f2\emdash\f1  Repair After Conflict\par
Key Message\par
Repair matters more than perfection.\par
\par
What This Means for Your Child\par
Conflict is normal.\par
\par
Apologising builds trust.\par
\par
Repair teaches emotional safety.\par
\par
Children learn from your example.\par
\par
Try This at Home\par
Say: \ldblquote I\rquote m sorry. I shouldn\rquote t have yelled.\rdblquote\par
\par
Reconnect with a hug or gentle touch.\par
\par
Talk about feelings after everyone is calm.\par
\par
Model healthy repair.\par
\par
Reflection Questions\par
How do I repair after conflict?\par
\par
What helps my child reconnect?\par
\par
What can I do differently next time?\par
\par
Notes\par
(Blank space)\par
\par
\f0\u-10179?\u-9000?\f1  EPISODE 12 \f2\emdash\f1  How Healing Happens\par
Key Message\par
Healing happens in small moments of connection.\par
\par
What This Means for Your Child\par
Safety rewires the brain.\par
\par
Play strengthens connection.\par
\par
Warmth and consistency matter.\par
\par
Progress is slow but real.\par
\par
Try This at Home\par
Spend 10 minutes of child\f3\_\f1 led play daily.\par
\par
Use warm eye contact and gentle touch.\par
\par
Celebrate small wins.\par
\par
Create predictable, safe moments.\par
\par
Reflection Questions\par
What small moments can I create today?\par
\par
How has my child grown recently?\par
\par
What helps us feel connected?\par
\par
Notes\par
